        <section id="the-elements-of-user-experience-(ux)-design" >
          
            <h2>The elements of User Experience (UX) design</h2>
              <p>The goal of a User experience (UX) project is to make sure that the end user has a positive experience with a product. To enable this to happen, the 5 elements of UX design acts as a guide for UX designers as it provides structure and direction to their decision making for any given project.</p>
<p>They were created by a UX designer called Jesse James Garrett. In the below Read item you can see the original diagram from his book which was published in 2000.</p>
<p>As can be seen in the diagram below the 5 elements of UX are on 5 separate planes. Each one is positioned on top of the previous one to create an outline of the full process from the bottom to the top.</p>
<p><img src="~/images/svg/cp3/cp3_u2_s5_p1_ux_wideimage.svg" alt=""></p>

          
        </section>
        
    
     
    
  
     
    
  
  
        <section id="the-five-elements-of-ux-design"  class="t-bg-primary">
          
            <h2>The five elements of UX design</h2>
              <p>Scroll through the slides below to learn more about the five elements of UX design.</p>

              
              
<div class="gallery-container">
  <div role="group" class="gallery no-js">
    <ul>
        <li role="listitem">
          
    <div class="l-media ">
        
        <h2 class="l-media__title">
          Strategy
        </h2>

      <div class="l-media__body ">
        <p>Strategy is the bottom plane of the model.  This is the most abstract and has the least constraints.  It is where the decisions are made to determine the objectives of the project and how they should be met with the design. The objectives would include the goals of the clients and stakeholders, and they would decide at this stage why the site is needed, how it will be used, and who will be using it.</p>

      </div>
          <img class="l-media__img" src="~/images/svg/cp3/cp3_u2_s5_p1_slide1.svg" alt="" width="300" height="300" >  
    </div>
        </li>
        <li role="listitem">
          
    <div class="l-media ">
        
        <h2 class="l-media__title">
          Scope
        </h2>

      <div class="l-media__body ">
        <p>The scope of the product can be determined once the strategy has been decided.  It is on this plane that the product features, content, and functionality are decided upon. It will include the requirements for the functions and features in the product and how they would work together.  Every single feature and list of content requirements of the product would be included at this stage. The requirements would be aligned with the project’s strategy.</p>

      </div>
          <img class="l-media__img" src="~/images/svg/cp3/cp3_u2_s5_p1_slide2.svg" alt="" width="300" height="300" >  
    </div>
        </li>
        <li role="listitem">
          
    <div class="l-media ">
        
        <h2 class="l-media__title">
          Structure
        </h2>

      <div class="l-media__body ">
        <p>The structure of the product can be worked on once the scope has been outlined.  This is where information architecture (IA) and  interaction design would be considered. IA is the practice of deciding how to arrange the parts of the product (structure) so it can be understood. Interaction design is how users will interact with the site and how the system will respond. This would include deciding each element of navigation.</p>

      </div>
          <img class="l-media__img" src="~/images/svg/cp3/cp3_u2_s5_p1_slide3.svg" alt="" width="300" height="300" >  
    </div>
        </li>
        <li role="listitem">
          
    <div class="l-media ">
        
        <h2 class="l-media__title">
          Skeleton
        </h2>

      <div class="l-media__body ">
        <p>Once the structure has been defined the skeleton of the site (also known as a wireframe) can be designed. It would be decided where the navigation and functional elements (which were determined from the previous plane) would be placed for each product page.  Wireframes and prototypes would be created at this stage to arrange each part of the product e.g. buttons, images, text etc.  Interface design (ID), Navigation design, and Information design would be considered on this plane.</p>

      </div>
          <img class="l-media__img" src="~/images/svg/cp3/cp3_u2_s5_p1_slide4.svg" alt="" width="300" height="300" >  
    </div>
        </li>
        <li role="listitem">
          
    <div class="l-media ">
        
        <h2 class="l-media__title">
          Surface
        </h2>

      <div class="l-media__body ">
        <p>The wireframes and prototype that was created on the Skeleton plane would be used on the final Surface plane.  The Surface plane is the top and most concrete plan and this is where the final pages for the product are created. Users’ sensory experience is considered at this stage and would include how colour and textures within the visual design would help them to navigate and interact with the site.</p>

      </div>
          <img class="l-media__img" src="~/images/svg/cp3/cp3_u2_s5_p1_slide5.svg" alt="" width="300" height="300" >  
    </div>
        </li>
    </ul>
  </div>
</div>

            
          
        </section>
        
    
     
    
  
     
    
  
  
